>
  <p>
    A few years ago I mentioned to a new neighbor that I write romance novels. She lit up — she reads a lot of
    romance herself — and invited me to a book event that, it turned out, was <em>very</em> far from what I write.
    My husband had been introducing me around the neighborhood as "my wife writes closed door romance, or clean
    romance," and somewhere along the way he just stopped using the first term. Almost nobody outside the romance
    community actually knows what "closed door" means. Half the time, "clean" needs explaining too.
  </p>
  <p>
    So this is the article I wish I could just hand people: what these terms actually mean, where they overlap and
    where they don't, and how to find clean romance that fits what <em>you</em> specifically want — not just what
    the label on the cover implies.
  </p>

  <h2>What does "clean romance" actually mean?</h2>
  <p>
    At its core, clean romance is romance written without explicit sexual content on the page, and generally without
    strong profanity. Readers might get real chemistry, tension, and a great kiss — but the story doesn't take you
    into the bedroom, and it doesn't rely on graphic language to make a scene land.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's the short version, and it holds up for the vast majority of what gets shelved as clean romance. But if
    you go looking for a single, universally agreed-upon definition — an actual industry standard everyone signs off
    on — you won't find one. Readers and authors have been debating the exact edges of "clean" for years, because
    there's no equivalent of a movie rating board for romance novels. Amazon eventually created its own "Clean &amp;
    Wholesome Romance" category to give the term some retail structure, which tells you two things at once: enough
    readers wanted it to matter, and no existing category was doing the job cleanly enough on its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    In practice, that means "clean" is doing a lot of work as an umbrella term. It's less a strict rulebook and more
    a signal — a way of telling a reader, before they buy, roughly what kind of experience they're getting.
  </p>

  <h2>"Clean," "closed door," and "fade to black" aren't quite the same word</h2>
  <p>
    These three terms get used almost interchangeably online, and I understand why — they're circling the same
    general idea. But they're not identical, and the differences actually matter if you're trying to find something
    specific.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Closed door</strong> is the term you'll hear most from readers and authors, and it's also the most
    misleading one, because the name itself implies something it usually doesn't mean. "Closed door" sounds like
    there's a door — like intimacy is happening, just off-page, and the story simply cuts away before showing it.
    That's the reading most people default to. But in practice, most books marketed as closed door don't have any
    door to close at all: there's no sexual intimacy between the characters, on the page or off. The term is really
    describing what you won't see on the page, not making a claim about what's happening in the story's world — it
    just doesn't sound that way, which is exactly why so many readers (and plenty of people outside the romance
    community entirely) end up assuming the wrong thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fade to black</strong> is a narrower, more literal version of the same idea — the scene is clearly
    heading somewhere, and the narration cuts away right as things get intimate, then picks back up after. It
    implies something did happen; the camera just looked away. Not every closed-door book uses this technique, and
    not every clean romance has anything to fade away from in the first place.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Clean</strong> is the broadest of the three. Readers generally use it as a wider content signal — folding
    in language and tone alongside what is or isn't shown on the page — where "closed door" is really just talking
    about the handling of sexual content specifically. A book can be closed door and still have language or content
    some clean-romance readers wouldn't consider "clean." That's exactly why relying on just one of these words can
    leave you with the wrong book in your hands.
  </p>

  <EditorialCallout eyebrow="Worth knowing" title="A closed-door label isn't a full content guide">
    <p>
      Because "closed door" is specifically about sexual content, it doesn't automatically tell you anything about
      profanity, innuendo, violence, or other things you might be sensitive to. Two closed-door books can land in
      very different places on everything else. If those details matter to you, it's worth checking for an author's
      note or content notes rather than assuming the label covers it.
    </p>
  </EditorialCallout>

  <h2>What about "no spice"?</h2>
  <p>
    "Spice" has become the go-to shorthand for a book's heat level, largely thanks to reader communities on
    BookTok and Bookstagram rating books on an informal pepper or chili scale. "No spice" simply means a book sits
    at the bottom of that scale — little to no sexual content. It's genuinely useful reader shorthand, but it's
    worth knowing it's exactly that: an informal, crowd-sourced label, not a formal publishing classification the
    way a retailer category is. You'll see "no spice" used by readers to describe books that would also be called
    clean or closed door — the terms overlap heavily in practice, even though they came from different corners of
    the reading community.
  </p>

  <PullQuote attribution="Tiffany Noelle Chacon">
    People think "closed door" means I'm writing something explicit and just cutting away from it. Usually it means
    the opposite — there's nothing back there to cut away from.
  </PullQuote>

  <h2>Tiffany's own promise</h2>
  <p>
    All of that is genre terminology — useful for describing the wider clean-romance shelf, but not a description of
    any one author's books. So to be specific about mine: my reader promise is <strong>Kisses Only. No explicit
    scenes. Ever.</strong> Not "usually." Not "mostly closed door with one exception." Every book I publish holds to
    that line, along with keeping the page free of profanity.
  </p>
  <p>
    That doesn't mean my books avoid real stakes. A number of my stories deal with genuinely heavy things — grief,
    trauma, family estrangement, the kind of fear that follows an accident — because clean doesn't mean shallow. It
    means the romance itself stays kisses-only while the rest of the story is allowed to be as honest as it needs to
    be. If a book of mine touches on something sensitive, I try to flag it plainly rather than let a reader discover
    it cold.
  </p>

  <h2>What "clean" might not tell you — and how to actually find your fit</h2>
  <p>
    Because none of these terms are policed by any single standard, the most reliable way to find clean romance that
    actually matches what you want isn't to trust the label alone. A few things that help:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the specific promise, not just the shelf.</strong> "Clean" on a retailer category page can mean
      several different things depending on who applied it. An author's own stated content promise (like mine above)
      is more precise than the genre label.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Decide what you actually mean by "clean."</strong> Are you specifically avoiding explicit scenes? Also
      avoiding profanity? Looking for zero on-page intimacy versus fade-to-black? Knowing your own answer makes it
      much easier to tell whether a given book fits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Look for content or trigger notes</strong> when a topic matters to you specifically — grief, violence,
      past trauma, and similar subjects can appear in a clean romance without any conflict with "clean" as a rating,
      since that term is about explicit content and language, not subject matter.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
